WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
Radiometric thermal sensors record measurement data for pixels or defined areas within

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.030
stated limits.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.400
Non-radiometric sensors primarily provide relative visual contrast.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.855
Both can support observation,

00:00:10.855 --> 00:00:14.800
but only appropriate radiometric systems should be used for qualified temperature

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:15.910
measurement.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.135
Resolution affects spatial detail,

00:00:17.135 --> 00:00:21.180
while thermal sensitivity describes the sensor's ability to distinguish small temperature

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.250
differences under specified conditions.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.040
Refresh rate affects motion rendering.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.977
None of these specifications alone guarantees a useful inspection.

00:00:28.977 --> 00:00:31.456
Sensitivity is not the same as absolute measurement accuracy.

00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:35.180
A sensor may display subtle contrast while the numerical value still depends on

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:35.920
calibration,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.220
target coverage, emissivity assumptions, reflected radiation, range,

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.840
and environmental conditions.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.640
Lens field of view controls scene coverage and target size.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.440
A wider view covers more area but places fewer pixels on a small target.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.585
A narrower view can improve target detail from an appropriate distance while reducing

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:52.270
context.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.280
Calibration supports consistency between detector response and reported values.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.371
Some systems perform internal correction automatically, but calibration status, warm-up,

00:01:00.371 --> 00:01:02.680
range, and environmental assumptions still matter.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.304
Follow manufacturer and organizational procedures.

00:01:05.304 --> 00:01:09.610
Drone-mounted payloads add vibration, movement, changing angle, altitude, wind,

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.440
and flight-safety constraints.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.520
The aircraft must carry the payload safely, preserve required clearance,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.280
and collect enough stable target pixels for the mission.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.840
Match capability to the question.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.785
Screening for broad anomalies, measuring a small electrical connection, mapping a roof,

00:01:22.785 --> 00:01:26.475
and supporting fire awareness have different sensor, lens, distance, timing,

00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:28.115
and expertise requirements.

00:01:28.115 --> 00:01:30.480
Define acceptable uncertainty before selecting equipment.
